    "q-139125": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "The displacement $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your final answer to the nearest Newton.",
      "worked_solution": "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot ⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18t,\\,36{t}^{2}-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =⟨18(4),\\,36({4}^{2})-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨72,\\,570⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(4)| & =\\sqrt{(72)^{2}+{(570)}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈575\\,\\text{N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest Newton.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
