    "q-50971": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Which of the following series converge?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14212321/assets/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n}{n+2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series I converges.",
