    "q-124842": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 52",
      "problem": "Given the series $S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}+3\\sqrt{n}}{2{n}^{4}},$ which of the following is true?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that $S$ is not a $p$-series, and we know from the $n$th term test the condition $\\frac{{n}^{3}+3\\sqrt{n}}{2{n}^{4}}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ as $n\\to \\infty $ does not tell us anything about the convergence or divergence of the series.\n\nThe comparison test for divergence states the following:\n\n*Consider two series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are both positive and ${a}_{n}\\ge {b}_{n}$ for all $n.$ If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.*\n\nWe follow four steps to show that $S$ is divergent.\n\n**Step 1**: First, we let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}+3\\sqrt{n}}{2{n}^{4}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{2n}.$ We note that ${a}_{n}>0$ and ${b}_{n}>0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\n**Step 2**: We show that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent. We have,\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nwhich is divergent by the $p$-series test.\n\n**Step 3**: We show that ${a}_{n}\\ge {b}_{n}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & \\overset{?}{\\ge }{b}_{n} \\\\ \\frac{{n}^{3}+3\\sqrt{n}}{2{n}^{4}} & \\ge \\frac{{n}^{3}}{2{n}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{2n}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We conclude that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent by the comparison test.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
