    "q-337709": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 53",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)y(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}xy+y \\\\ -2x-y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+y)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+y)=x+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x-y)=-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & x+1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-(-2)(1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1)+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
